<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daifang Commandery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 204–314</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daifang was a Chinese commandery in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, in the area of modern Hwanghae, carved out of Lelang by the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-kang">Gongsun Kang</a> around 204. Later held by Wei and Jin, it was a centre of contact with the Han peoples of southern Korea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wa-japan">Wa of Japan</a> until pressure from Goguryeo and Baekje ended it around 314.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daifang-commandery.jpg" alt="Daifang Commandery" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_4th_century_Daifang_commandery.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
